Hecks.bluebook "HecksagonIr", version: "2026.05.21.1" do
  vision "The .hecksagon DSL describes its own shape. Phase 1 of i695 : lift the Rust kernel IR (rust/src/hecksagon_ir.rs) into a bluebook so the contract that today lives only in Rust struct definitions becomes a first-class domain that the runtime can parse, the validators can check, and the macrophages can keep honest. Phase 2 (separate card) regenerates the Rust IR from this bluebook ; this Phase ONLY conceives the bluebook contract — the existing Rust file is untouched."
  category "framework"

  # ============================================================
  # HECKSAGON IR — the .hecksagon DSL as a bluebook
  # ============================================================
  #
  # First chapter, 2026-05-21, i695 Phase 1. Until now, .hecksagon
  # files have been parsed BY hand-written Rust into the structs
  # at rust/src/hecksagon_ir.rs. The Rust file IS the IR contract,
  # the parser, and the only documentation of the IR shape — all
  # at once. This bluebook flips the relationship :
  #
  #   * The bluebook becomes the contract.
  #   * The Rust parser becomes one (the first) implementation.
  #   * Phase 2 (sibling card) wires the parser to regen from this
  #     bluebook so the Rust struct shape can never drift from the
  #     bluebook again.
  #
  # ── What this bluebook IS, vs. what it isn't ──
  #
  # IS : the shape of a parsed .hecksagon file. Names, persistence,
  # adapter lists, gates, subscriptions. The IR — the canonical
  # in-memory form the runtime walks.
  #
  # ISN'T : the .hecksagon DSL syntax (that's the parser's job),
  # the dispatcher (runtime/), or the adapter family registry
  # (framework/adapter_families/*.hecksagon). Those are sibling
  # surfaces ; this bluebook describes only the IR they produce
  # and consume.
  #
  # ── One aggregate per Rust struct ──
  #
  # The Rust file contains seven structs : Hecksagon (root),
  # IoAdapter, ShellAdapter, Gate, LlmAdapter, ComputeAdapter,
  # TtsAdapter. This bluebook conceives one aggregate per struct.
  # Each carries the struct's fields as typed value-objects (per
  # the no-primitive-envy discipline, i555) and a Conceive command
  # that mirrors the struct's default constructor : every field
  # the parser writes at construction time is a Conceive attribute.
  #
  # ── Multi-trigger : the one structural lift over Rust today ──
  #
  # The Rust IR carries `trigger_on: Option<String>` on
  # LlmAdapter, ComputeAdapter, TtsAdapter. The bluebook lifts
  # this to `triggers: list_of(TriggerOn)` so a single adapter
  # can fire on more than one upstream command. The 1-element
  # list IS the historical single-trigger case ; the empty list
  # means "no explicit trigger ; fall back to response_into_target
  # for Llm/Compute, no-fire for Tts" (same fallback semantics
  # the Rust impl carries today through `effective_trigger`).
  # Phase 2 widens the Rust parser to accept both forms
  # (single string + list) and Phase 3 collapses Option<String>
  # into Vec<String>. This bluebook ONLY declares the new shape ;
  # the parser catches up in the sibling cards.
  #
  # ── Why not one mega-aggregate ──
  #
  # The Rust root struct (Hecksagon) carries Vec<IoAdapter>,
  # Vec<ShellAdapter>, etc. — each adapter type is its own
  # struct. Bluebooking each as a sibling aggregate (rather
  # than child entities inside one giant Hecksagon aggregate)
  # matches both the Rust source organisation and the test
  # surface : each Conceive smoke-test exercises one adapter
  # type in isolation. The Hecksagon root aggregate carries
  # the scalar fields (name, framework_kind, persistence) plus
  # subscriptions as a list_of(Subscription) ; the adapter lists
  # belong to the file-as-a-whole, not to the root aggregate's
  # state — they're discovered by the parser per-aggregate-kind
  # and merged into the runtime's lookup tables.
  #
  # ── Companion files ───────────────────────────────────────────
  #
  # hecksagon_ir.behaviors — one Conceive smoke test per aggregate.
  # hecksagon_ir.hecksagon — adapter :memory per aggregate.
  # rust/src/hecksagon_ir.rs — the IR struct file this bluebook
  #                            mirrors. Phase 2 regenerates that
  #                            file from this bluebook ; Phase 1
  #                            leaves it alone.

  # ============================================================
  # HECKSAGON — root of a parsed .hecksagon file
  # ============================================================
  #
  # Mirror of Hecksagon::Structure::Hecksagon (Ruby) and
  # rust/src/hecksagon_ir.rs Hecksagon struct (Rust). Holds the
  # file's scalar fields ; the adapter lists ARE per-aggregate
  # sibling collections, discovered by the parser and merged
  # by the runtime — not state of this aggregate.
  aggregate "Hecksagon", "Root of a parsed .hecksagon file. Carries the file's name, optional framework_kind discriminator (for meta-layer files), optional persistence adapter, and the cross-domain subscription list. Adapter declarations (shell, llm, compute, tts, io, gates) live on their own sibling aggregates ; the parser produces one Conceive per adapter found." do
    identified_by :name

    # Name — the string passed to `Hecks.hecksagon "Name" do`.
    value_object "Name" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # FrameworkKind — the meta-layer discriminator. Plain
    # `Hecks.hecksagon` files leave this empty ; files declared
    # via `Hecks.adapter_family` / `Hecks.provider` /
    # `Hecks.behavior_kind` carry the family-kind string here.
    # The kernel framework_registry walks framework/* and indexes
    # by this value.
    value_object "FrameworkKind" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # Persistence — "memory" / "heki" / "" for the runtime
    # default. Mirror of Hecksagon.persistence: Option<String>.
    # The empty-string case IS the None case.
    value_object "Persistence" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # Subscription — one entry from `subscribe "OtherDomain"`.
    # Each subscription is a directed edge into the runtime so
    # cross-domain policy routing can fire.
    value_object "Subscription" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    attribute :name,           Name
    attribute :framework_kind, FrameworkKind
    attribute :persistence,    Persistence
    attribute :subscriptions,  list_of(Subscription)

    command "Conceive" do
      role "Framework"
      description "Bring a Hecksagon root record into being from a parsed .hecksagon file. Inputs : the file's declared name, an optional framework_kind discriminator (empty string for plain files), an optional persistence string (\"memory\" / \"heki\" / empty for the runtime default), and the subscription list. Adapter declarations are sibling Conceive dispatches against the IoAdapter / ShellAdapter / LlmAdapter / ComputeAdapter / TtsAdapter / Gate aggregates."
      attribute :name,           Name
      attribute :framework_kind, FrameworkKind
      attribute :persistence,    Persistence
      attribute :subscriptions,  list_of(Subscription)
      emits "Conceived"
    end
  end

  # ============================================================
  # IO ADAPTER — :stdout / :stderr / :stdin / :env / :fs / etc.
  # ============================================================
  #
  # Mirror of rust/src/hecksagon_ir.rs IoAdapter. Carries whatever
  # options the parser extracts ; the runtime decides what each
  # one means.
  aggregate "IoAdapter", "Non-persistence adapter binding (:stdout, :stderr, :stdin, :env, :fs, etc.). Carries the adapter kind (without the leading colon), the option key/value pairs lifted from the inline form or block body, and optional on-event hooks the runtime uses as policy-style triggers." do
    identified_by :name

    # Name — synthesised identity for the parsed record (e.g.
    # "stdout" / "stderr" / "env" — the kind doubles as the
    # natural key when only one binding of that kind exists,
    # which is the common case).
    value_object "Name" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # Kind — the adapter symbol with the leading colon stripped.
    value_object "Kind" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # OptionEntry — one (key, value) pair from the options hash.
    # Carries two flat strings ; the runtime interprets them.
    value_object "OptionEntry" do
      attribute :key,   String
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # OnEvent — one event name from an `on :Event do … end` hook
    # inside the adapter block.
    value_object "OnEvent" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    attribute :name,       Name
    attribute :kind,       Kind
    attribute :options,    list_of(OptionEntry)
    attribute :on_events,  list_of(OnEvent)

    command "Conceive" do
      role "Framework"
      description "Record an IoAdapter parsed from a .hecksagon file. Inputs : a synthesised name (typically the kind), the kind string (without leading colon), the options key/value pairs, and the on-event hook list."
      attribute :name,       Name
      attribute :kind,       Kind
      attribute :options,    list_of(OptionEntry)
      attribute :on_events,  list_of(OnEvent)
      emits "Conceived"
    end
  end

  # ============================================================
  # SHELL ADAPTER — `adapter :shell, name:, command:, args:, …`
  # ============================================================
  #
  # Mirror of Hecksagon::Structure::ShellAdapter and
  # rust/src/hecksagon_ir.rs ShellAdapter. Execution semantics
  # live in the runtime's ShellDispatcher
  # (runtime/shell_dispatcher.rs).
  aggregate "ShellAdapter", "Named shell-command binding declared with `adapter :shell, name:, command:, args:, output_format:, timeout:, working_dir:, env:, ok_exit:`. Args may carry `{{placeholder}}` tokens that the dispatcher substitutes from the triggering command's attrs ; the placeholders helper walks args in first-appearance order. Execution lives in the runtime's ShellDispatcher." do
    identified_by :name

    # Name — the adapter's natural-key identifier (the `name:`
    # keyword argument on the DSL form).
    value_object "Name" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # Command — the shell command (the executable / first word).
    value_object "Command" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # Arg — one positional argument. May contain
    # `{{placeholder}}` tokens.
    value_object "Arg" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # OutputFormat — closed set : "text", "lines", "json",
    # "json_lines", "exit_code". The dispatcher decodes stdout
    # according to this value.
    value_object "OutputFormat" do
      attribute :value, String, enum: ["text", "lines", "json", "json_lines", "exit_code"]
    end

    # Timeout — optional wall-clock cap in milliseconds, as a
    # string (empty for unbounded). String-shaped so the IR
    # round-trips clean ; the dispatcher parses on use.
    value_object "Timeout" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # WorkingDir — optional cwd for the shelled command (empty
    # for the runtime's default).
    value_object "WorkingDir" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # EnvEntry — one (name, value) pair appended to the shelled
    # command's environment.
    value_object "EnvEntry" do
      attribute :key,   String
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # OkExit — the expected success exit code as a string (empty
    # for "0"). When output_format == "exit_code" the dispatcher
    # treats every exit code as success.
    value_object "OkExit" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    attribute :name,          Name
    attribute :command,       Command
    attribute :args,          list_of(Arg)
    attribute :output_format, OutputFormat
    attribute :timeout,       Timeout
    attribute :working_dir,   WorkingDir
    attribute :env,           list_of(EnvEntry)
    attribute :ok_exit,       OkExit

    command "Conceive" do
      role "Framework"
      description "Record a ShellAdapter parsed from a .hecksagon file. Inputs : the adapter name, the command, the args list (`{{placeholder}}` tokens preserved), the output_format (one of the closed enum), the optional timeout / working_dir, the env key/value pairs, and the ok_exit string. Mirrors the ShellAdapter struct's default-constructor shape."
      attribute :name,          Name
      attribute :command,       Command
      attribute :args,          list_of(Arg)
      attribute :output_format, OutputFormat
      attribute :timeout,       Timeout
      attribute :working_dir,   WorkingDir
      attribute :env,           list_of(EnvEntry)
      attribute :ok_exit,       OkExit
      emits "Conceived"
    end
  end

  # ============================================================
  # GATE — `gate "Aggregate", :role do allow :Cmd end`
  # ============================================================
  #
  # Mirror of Hecksagon::Structure::GateDefinition and
  # rust/src/hecksagon_ir.rs Gate. Lists the commands a given
  # role is allowed to invoke against a given aggregate. The
  # runtime checks this list when dispatching.
  aggregate "Gate", "Role-gated allowlist for an aggregate. Declared with `gate \"Aggregate\", :role do allow :Cmd, :Cmd2 end`. Commands not in the allowed list are rejected by the bus when the dispatcher's role string matches `role`." do
    identified_by :name

    # Name — synthesised identity (typically "<Aggregate>:<role>").
    # Same shape conceptually as a tuple (aggregate, role) but
    # flattened to a single string for the natural-key surface.
    value_object "Name" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # Aggregate — the aggregate the gate guards.
    value_object "Aggregate" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # Role — the role the gate applies to (the bus's role string).
    value_object "Role" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # AllowedCommand — one command name from the `allow` list.
    value_object "AllowedCommand" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    attribute :name,             Name
    attribute :aggregate,        Aggregate
    attribute :role,             Role
    attribute :allowed_commands, list_of(AllowedCommand)

    command "Conceive" do
      role "Framework"
      description "Record a Gate parsed from a .hecksagon file. Inputs : the synthesised name (typically \"<Aggregate>:<role>\"), the aggregate the gate guards, the role string, and the list of allowed command names. Commands not in the list are rejected by the bus when the dispatcher's role string matches."
      attribute :name,             Name
      attribute :aggregate,        Aggregate
      attribute :role,             Role
      attribute :allowed_commands, list_of(AllowedCommand)
      emits "Conceived"
    end
  end

  # ============================================================
  # LLM ADAPTER — `adapter :llm, name:, prompt_template:, …`
  # ============================================================
  #
  # Mirror of Hecksagon::Structure::LlmAdapter and
  # rust/src/hecksagon_ir.rs LlmAdapter. The Phase 1 IR holds the
  # declared shape ; the runtime substitutes the prompt template
  # at dispatch time, fires the model, and routes the response
  # into the named command as the named attribute.
  #
  # ── The triggers lift ──
  #
  # Rust today : `trigger_on: Option<String>` — one optional
  # trigger target. Bluebook tomorrow : `triggers: list_of(TriggerOn)`
  # — zero or more. The 1-element list IS the historical case ;
  # zero means "fall back to response_into_target" (matches the
  # Rust `effective_trigger` fallback). Phase 2 widens the parser
  # to accept both forms ; Phase 3 collapses Option<String> into
  # Vec<String>. This bluebook ONLY declares the new shape.
  aggregate "LlmAdapter", "Named LLM-call binding declared with `adapter :llm, name:, prompt_template:, model:, max_tokens:, response_into:, backend:, …`. Carries the prompt template (with `{{placeholder}}` tokens), the model identifier, max-tokens budget, the triggers list (zero, one, or many upstream dispatch targets that fire the adapter), the response routing target and attribute name, and the optional backend (:claude / :ollama / :fixture). When triggers is empty the runtime falls back to response_into_target as the firing target." do
    identified_by :name

    # Name — the adapter's natural-key identifier.
    value_object "Name" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # PromptTemplate — the prompt body with `{{placeholder}}`
    # tokens the dispatcher substitutes from the triggering
    # command's attrs.
    value_object "PromptTemplate" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # Model — model identifier (e.g. "claude-3-5-sonnet-latest",
    # "gpt-4o"). Empty for the backend's default.
    value_object "Model" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # MaxTokens — the response token budget as a string (empty
    # for the backend's default). String-shaped so the IR
    # round-trips clean ; the dispatcher parses on use.
    value_object "MaxTokens" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # TriggerOn — one dispatch target ("Aggregate.Command") the
    # adapter fires on. The list_of(TriggerOn) attribute is the
    # i695 lift over Rust's single Option<String> — zero, one,
    # or many triggers.
    value_object "TriggerOn" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # ResponseIntoTarget — the "Aggregate.Command" path the
    # response cascades into.
    value_object "ResponseIntoTarget" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # ResponseIntoAttr — the receiving attribute name on the
    # response_into_target.
    value_object "ResponseIntoAttr" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # Backend — :claude / :ollama / :fixture (without leading colon).
    value_object "Backend" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    attribute :name,                 Name
    attribute :prompt_template,      PromptTemplate
    attribute :model,                Model
    attribute :max_tokens,           MaxTokens
    attribute :triggers,             list_of(TriggerOn)
    attribute :response_into_target, ResponseIntoTarget
    attribute :response_into_attr,   ResponseIntoAttr
    attribute :backend,              Backend

    command "Conceive" do
      role "Framework"
      description "Record an LlmAdapter parsed from a .hecksagon file. Inputs : the adapter name, the prompt template (with `{{placeholder}}` tokens), the model identifier (empty for backend default), the max_tokens budget as a string (empty for backend default), the triggers list (zero entries falls back to response_into_target ; one entry is the historical single-trigger case ; many entries fan in from multiple upstream commands), the response_into_target / response_into_attr pair, and the optional backend symbol (without leading colon)."
      attribute :name,                 Name
      attribute :prompt_template,      PromptTemplate
      attribute :model,                Model
      attribute :max_tokens,           MaxTokens
      attribute :triggers,             list_of(TriggerOn)
      attribute :response_into_target, ResponseIntoTarget
      attribute :response_into_attr,   ResponseIntoAttr
      attribute :backend,              Backend
      emits "Conceived"
    end
  end

  # ============================================================
  # COMPUTE ADAPTER — `adapter :compute, name:, function:, …`
  # ============================================================
  #
  # Mirror of rust/src/hecksagon_ir.rs ComputeAdapter. Sibling
  # of LlmAdapter for local computation : where :llm substitutes
  # a prompt and chains the model's response, :compute calls a
  # named built-in function (resolved through
  # runtime/compute_functions/) and chains the returned string
  # into response_into_target as response_into_attr.
  #
  # Same triggers lift as LlmAdapter — zero/one/many upstream
  # dispatch targets ; empty falls back to response_into_target.
  aggregate "ComputeAdapter", "Named local-computation binding declared with `adapter :compute, name:, function:, trigger_on:, response_into:, attr:`. The runtime resolves function_name through the compute_functions registry, invokes it with the upstream state + dispatch attrs, and chains the returned string into response_into_target under response_into_attr. Same triggers fallback semantics as LlmAdapter — empty list falls back to response_into_target." do
    identified_by :name

    value_object "Name" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # FunctionName — the registry key the runtime resolves to a
    # built-in function (e.g. "summarize_recent_musings").
    value_object "FunctionName" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    value_object "TriggerOn" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    value_object "ResponseIntoTarget" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    value_object "ResponseIntoAttr" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    attribute :name,                 Name
    attribute :function_name,        FunctionName
    attribute :triggers,             list_of(TriggerOn)
    attribute :response_into_target, ResponseIntoTarget
    attribute :response_into_attr,   ResponseIntoAttr

    command "Conceive" do
      role "Framework"
      description "Record a ComputeAdapter parsed from a .hecksagon file. Inputs : the adapter name, the function_name (registry key in runtime/compute_functions/), the triggers list (zero/one/many — empty falls back to response_into_target, matching LlmAdapter), and the response_into_target / response_into_attr pair."
      attribute :name,                 Name
      attribute :function_name,        FunctionName
      attribute :triggers,             list_of(TriggerOn)
      attribute :response_into_target, ResponseIntoTarget
      attribute :response_into_attr,   ResponseIntoAttr
      emits "Conceived"
    end
  end

  # ============================================================
  # TTS ADAPTER — `adapter :tts, name:, provider:, voice_id:, …`
  # ============================================================
  #
  # Mirror of rust/src/hecksagon_ir.rs TtsAdapter. The
  # text-to-speech adapter family (framework/adapter_families/
  # tts.hecksagon, behavior_kind render_text_to_audio). Fire-and-
  # forget : `response_field :none` on the family, so there's no
  # response_into / cascade ; effective_trigger returns
  # triggers-list-or-empty only (no fallback to response_into,
  # unlike Llm/Compute) because :tts chains into nothing.
  #
  # Same triggers lift as LlmAdapter/ComputeAdapter — zero/one/
  # many upstream targets, declared as a list.
  aggregate "TtsAdapter", "Named text-to-speech binding declared with `adapter :tts, name:, provider:, voice_id:, model:, speed:, stability:, similarity_boost:, style:, trigger_on:, cache_dir:, auto_play:`. Fire-and-forget — the family's response_field is :none, so there's no cascade. The runtime renders audio via the resolved provider. Same triggers lift as LlmAdapter (zero/one/many) but no response_into fallback — when triggers is empty the adapter does not fire." do
    identified_by :name

    value_object "Name" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # Provider — the TTS backend identifier (e.g. "elevenlabs",
    # "kokoro", "openai"). Empty for the family's default.
    value_object "Provider" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # VoiceId — the provider-specific voice identifier (empty for
    # the provider's default voice).
    value_object "VoiceId" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # Model — the provider-specific model identifier (empty for
    # the provider's default model).
    value_object "Model" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # Speed — synthesis speed as a string (empty for the
    # provider's default). String-shaped so the IR round-trips
    # clean ; the dispatcher parses on use.
    value_object "Speed" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # Stability — provider-specific stability parameter, as a
    # string (empty for the provider's default).
    value_object "Stability" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # SimilarityBoost — provider-specific similarity-boost
    # parameter, as a string (empty for the provider's default).
    value_object "SimilarityBoost" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # Style — provider-specific style parameter, as a string
    # (empty for the provider's default).
    value_object "Style" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # CacheDir — optional path where rendered audio is cached
    # (empty for the family's default cache location).
    value_object "CacheDir" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # AutoPlay — "true" / "false" / "" string. The dispatcher
    # parses to bool on use ; empty means the family's default.
    value_object "AutoPlay" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    value_object "TriggerOn" do
      attribute :value, String
    end

    attribute :name,              Name
    attribute :provider,          Provider
    attribute :voice_id,          VoiceId
    attribute :model,             Model
    attribute :speed,             Speed
    attribute :stability,         Stability
    attribute :similarity_boost,  SimilarityBoost
    attribute :style,             Style
    attribute :triggers,          list_of(TriggerOn)
    attribute :cache_dir,         CacheDir
    attribute :auto_play,         AutoPlay

    command "Conceive" do
      role "Framework"
      description "Record a TtsAdapter parsed from a .hecksagon file. Inputs : the adapter name, the provider / voice_id / model / speed / stability / similarity_boost / style / cache_dir / auto_play fields (each as a string ; empty means the provider's or family's default), and the triggers list (zero/one/many — when empty the adapter does not fire because :tts chains into nothing)."
      attribute :name,              Name
      attribute :provider,          Provider
      attribute :voice_id,          VoiceId
      attribute :model,             Model
      attribute :speed,             Speed
      attribute :stability,         Stability
      attribute :similarity_boost,  SimilarityBoost
      attribute :style,             Style
      attribute :triggers,          list_of(TriggerOn)
      attribute :cache_dir,         CacheDir
      attribute :auto_play,         AutoPlay
      emits "Conceived"
    end
  end
end
